{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JabKPhgTK1ATYaiy8K45GV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Abandon \"distributional fingerprint\" as primary contribution.\n2\t  Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) explain nearly all classifier variance; reference-model\n3\t  entropy statistics add negligible independent signal. Cross-generator generalization\n4\t  collapses because surface vocabulary statistics are generator-specific artifacts,\n5\t  not universal depth markers. Reframe paper as an empirical characterization of recursive\n6\t  generation dynamics: \"We tested H₁ vs H₂ and found that non-paraphrastic chains\n7\t  also converge rapidly in distributional space, with detectable differences confined\n8\t  primarily to vocabulary impoverishment.\" Reposition as negative-but-informative\n9\t  result extending attractor cycle theory to non-paraphrastic settings. New angle:\n10\t  vocabulary impoverishment as a universal but shallow fingerprint — theoretically\n11\t  motivated by generation entropy convergence under autoregressive sampling. Target\n12\t  AAAI as empirical science contribution with clear hypothesis-testing framing and\n13\t  boundary conditions for future work.\n14\t\n15\t  '\n16\timagined_experiments:\n17\t- name: feature_ablation\n18\t  plan_id: plan_001\n19\t  description: 'Ablation of reference-only, surface-only, and full feature sets on\n20\t    MVP data (Pythia-2.8B generator, Pythia-410M reference, 3-class depth). Ordinal\n21\t    logistic regression + random forest, 5-fold CV.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    surface_only_macro_f1: 0.82\n26\t    surface_only_cv_std: 0.015\n27\t    ref_only_macro_f1: 0.47\n28\t    ref_only_cv_std: 0.032\n29\t    full_macro_f1: 0.83\n30\t    full_cv_std: 0.014\n31\t    ref_minus_surface_delta: -0.35\n32\t    full_minus_surface_delta: 0.01\n33\t    permutation_importance_hapax_ratio: 0.31\n34\t    permutation_importance_ttr: 0.24\n35\t    permutation_importance_entropy_mean: 0.04\n36\t    permutation_importance_entropy_std: 0.03\n37\t    permutation_importance_tail_decay: 0.02\n38\t    ref_features_added_significance_p: 0.18\n39\t  result_rationale: 'Surface features alone recover 97.6% of full-model performance\n40\t    (0.82 vs 0.84 MVP). hapax_ratio and TTR dominate because recursive generation\n41\t    progressively depletes rare vocabulary — a mechanical consequence of autoregressive\n42\t    sampling from a fixed distribution. Reference-model entropy statistics show near-chance\n43\t    3-class performance (0.47) because the entropy distribution under the reference\n44\t    model converges rapidly across generation depths: by gen-1, the token-level surprise\n45\t    distribution is already close to its stationary form. Adding reference features\n46\t    to surface features yields only +0.01 F1 (not statistically significant, p=0.18\n47\t    on paired bootstrap), confirming that entropy_mean/std/min/max carry redundant\n48\t    information already captured by vocabulary depletion proxies. The \"distributional\n49\t    fingerprint\" framing collapses — what we detect is vocabulary impoverishment,\n50\t    not a deeper distributional shift.\n51\t\n52\t    '\n53\t  tables:\n54\t  - name: 'Table 2: Feature Ablation Results (3-class, MVP data)'\n55\t    columns:\n56\t    - Feature_Set\n57\t    - Macro_F1\n58\t    - Gen0_F1\n59\t    - Gen1_F1\n60\t    - Gen2plus_F1\n61\t    - CV_Std\n62\t    rows:\n63\t    - - surface_only\n64\t      - 0.82\n65\t      - 0.94\n66\t      - 0.75\n67\t      - 0.77\n68\t      - 0.015\n69\t    - - ref_only\n70\t      - 0.47\n71\t      - 0.58\n72\t      - 0.38\n73\t      - 0.44\n74\t      - 0.032\n75\t    - - full\n76\t      - 0.83\n77\t      - 0.95\n78\t      - 0.76\n79\t      - 0.78\n80\t      - 0.014\n81\t    - - chance\n82\t      - 0.33\n83\t      - 0.33\n84\t      - 0.33\n85\t      - 0.33\n86\t      - '-'\n87\t  - name: 'Table 3: Permutation Feature Importance (Full Model)'\n88\t    columns:\n89\t    - Feature\n90\t    - Importance\n91\t    - Std\n92\t    rows:\n93\t    - - hapax_ratio\n94\t      - 0.31\n95\t      - 0.04\n96\t    - - ttr\n97\t      - 0.24\n98\t      - 0.03\n99\t    - - entropy_mean\n100\t      - 0.04\n101\t      - 0.02\n102\t    - - entropy_std\n103\t      - 0.03\n104\t      - 0.01\n105\t    - - entropy_min\n106\t      - 0.02\n107\t      - 0.01\n108\t    - - entropy_max\n109\t      - 0.01\n110\t      - 0.01\n111\t    - - tail_decay\n112\t      - 0.02\n113\t      - 0.01\n114\t  figures_description:\n115\t  - 'Fig 2a: Violin plot of per-depth feature distributions. hapax_ratio shows clear\n116\t    monotonic decrease (gen-0 median ~0.42, gen-1 ~0.31, gen-2+ ~0.27). entropy_mean\n117\t    shows overlapping distributions with only gen-0 slightly separated.'\n118\t  - 'Fig 2b: ROC curves for each feature set. Surface-only and full curves nearly\n119\t    overlap (AUC 0.91 vs 0.92). Ref-only curve barely above diagonal for gen-1 vs\n120\t    gen-2+ (AUC 0.56).'\n121\t  - 'Fig 2c: Confusion matrices side-by-side. Ref-only model conflates gen-1 and gen-2+\n122\t    heavily (off-diagonal mass ~55%). Surface-only cleanly separates gen-0 but confuses\n123\t    gen-1/gen-2+ at ~25% rate.'\n124\t  analysis: 'Claim 2 is falsified. The ablation decisively shows that reference-model\n125\t    features carry no independent signal. The full model''s marginal +0.01 over surface-only\n126\t    is within noise. This result is consistent with the hypothesis that autoregressive\n127\t    generation entropy converges to a near-stationary distribution by gen-1, while\n128\t    vocabulary depletion (a surface-level phenomenon) continues to accumulate. The\n129\t    practical implication: a $0 feature (hapax_ratio) matches the expensive reference-model\n130\t    inference pipeline.\n131\t\n132\t    '\n133\t- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n134\t  plan_id: plan_002\n135\t  description: '5 generators (Pythia-2.8B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3.5-2B, Qwen3.5-0.8B, Pythia-1B).\n136\t    1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out CV\n137\t    for cross-generator generalization. 3-class depth classification.\n138\t\n139\t    '\n140\t  expected_results:\n141\t    within_generator_avg_macro_f1: 0.81\n142\t    leave_one_out_avg_macro_f1: 0.48\n143\t    leave_one_out_std: 0.07\n144\t    leave_one_out_gen0_f1: 0.68\n145\t    leave_one_out_gen1_f1: 0.38\n146\t    leave_one_out_gen2plus_f1: 0.42\n147\t    loo_held_out_pythia28b_f1: 0.52\n148\t    loo_held_out_olmo_f1: 0.44\n149\t    loo_held_out_qwen2b_f1: 0.43\n150\t    loo_held_out_qwen08b_f1: 0.5\n151\t    loo_held_out_pythia1b_f1: 0.53\n152\t    gen1_vs_gen2plus_binary_loo_f1: 0.54\n153\t    pooled_all_generators_macro_f1: 0.62\n154\t  result_rationale: 'Within-generator performance remains strong (~0.81) because each\n155\t    generator has a characteristic vocabulary depletion rate that surface features\n156\t    learn. But leave-one-out drops catastrophically to 0.48 because the absolute levels\n157\t    of hapax_ratio and TTR are generator-specific: Qwen models produce systematically\n158\t    different type-token ratios than Pythia models at every depth. The classifier\n159\t    learns generator-specific thresholds, not universal depth signatures. Gen-0 identification\n160\t    is partially salvaged (0.68) because human-written text has a consistently distinct\n161\t    surface profile regardless of which generator produces the synthetic continuations.\n162\t    Gen-1 vs gen-2+ discrimination is near chance (0.38/0.42) in the cross-generator\n163\t    setting because the vocabulary depletion curves of different generators at depth\n164\t    1 overlap with other generators at depth 2+. Pythia-family held-out sets perform\n165\t    slightly better (0.52-0.53) due to Pythia-1B sharing vocabulary statistics with\n166\t    Pythia-2.8B in the training fold. Cross- family transfer (Qwen held out) is worst\n167\t    (0.43-0.44). This confirms that surface features are generator-specific, not universal\n168\t    depth markers.\n169\t\n170\t    '\n171\t  tables:\n172\t  - name: 'Table 4: Cross-Generator Generalization (Leave-One-Out)'\n173\t    columns:\n174\t    - Held_Out_Generator\n175\t    - Macro_F1\n176\t    - Gen0_F1\n177\t    - Gen1_F1\n178\t    - Gen2plus_F1\n179\t    rows:\n180\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n181\t      - 0.52\n182\t      - 0.71\n183\t      - 0.4\n184\t      - 0.45\n185\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n186\t      - 0.44\n187\t      - 0.65\n188\t      - 0.34\n189\t      - 0.38\n190\t    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n191\t      - 0.43\n192\t      - 0.64\n193\t      - 0.35\n194\t      - 0.39\n195\t    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n196\t      - 0.5\n197\t      - 0.7\n198\t      - 0.38\n199\t      - 0.43\n200\t    - - Pythia-1B\n201\t      - 0.53\n202\t      - 0.72\n203\t      - 0.42\n204\t      - 0.46\n205\t    - - '**Average**'\n206\t      - '**0.48**'\n207\t      - '**0.68**'\n208\t      - '**0.38**'\n209\t      - '**0.42**'\n210\t  - name: 'Table 5: Within- vs Cross-Generator Comparison'\n211\t    columns:\n212\t    - Setting\n213\t    - Macro_F1\n214\t    - Δ_vs_Within\n215\t    rows:\n216\t    - - within_generator_avg\n217\t      - 0.81\n218\t      - '-'\n219\t    - - leave_one_out_avg\n220\t      - 0.48\n221\t      - -0.33\n222\t    - - pooled_all_generators\n223\t      - 0.62\n224\t      - -0.19\n225\t  figures_description:\n226\t  - 'Fig 3a: Per-generator hapax_ratio distributions at each depth. Each generator\n227\t    occupies a distinct band — Qwen3.5-2B gen-0 median overlaps with Pythia-2.8B gen-1\n228\t    median, explaining cross-generator confusion.'\n229\t  - 'Fig 3b: Heatmap of leave-one-out F1 (held-out generator × depth class). Clear\n230\t    gradient: gen-0 row is yellow (decent), gen-1 and gen-2+ rows are dark red (near-chance).'\n231\t  - 'Fig 3c: t-SNE of feature space colored by generator. Points cluster by generator\n232\t    first, depth second — generator identity dominates the feature space geometry.'\n233\t  analysis: 'Claim 1 fails in its generalization requirement. Cross-generator macro-F1\n234\t    of 0.48 is barely above chance (0.33). The fundamental problem: surface vocabulary\n235\t    statistics are generator-specific calibration artifacts. A model that learns \"hapax_ratio\n236\t    < 0.30 means gen-2+\" from Pythia data will misclassify Qwen gen-0 text (which\n237\t    may have hapax_ratio ~0.35 vs Pythia gen-0 ~0.45). Without independent reference-model\n238\t    signal (which plan_001 showed is absent), there is no generator-invariant depth\n239\t    feature. The pooled setting (0.62) is inflated by within-generator signal bleeding\n240\t    through the non-held-out generators in training.\n241\t\n242\t    '\n243\t- name: cross_family_reference_model\n244\t  plan_id: plan_003\n245\t  description: 'Test OLMo-2-1B and Qwen3.5-0.8B as reference models (replacing Pythia-410M)\n246\t    for feature extraction. Full cross of 3 reference models × 5 generators.\n247\t\n248\t    '\n249\t  expected_results:\n250\t    pythia_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.48\n251\t    olmo_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.45\n252\t    qwen_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.44\n253\t    best_ref_model: pythia_410m\n254\t    ref_model_variance_std: 0.02\n255\t    ensemble_3ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.5\n256\t    ref_only_olmo_macro_f1: 0.43\n257\t    ref_only_qwen_macro_f1: 0.41\n258\t  result_rationale: 'Changing the reference model does not rescue cross-generator\n259\t    performance because the bottleneck is not reference model choice — it is the absence\n260\t    of depth-discriminative signal in the entropy statistics themselves. All three\n261\t    reference models produce similarly uninformative entropy distributions because\n262\t    the phenomenon is fundamental: autoregressive generation entropy converges to\n263\t    near-stationary levels by gen-1 regardless of which model measures it. The slight\n264\t    advantage of Pythia-410M (0.48 vs 0.44-0.45) is a minor architectural compatibility\n265\t    effect with the Pythia generators in the training set, not genuine signal. Ensembling\n266\t    three reference models yields only +0.02 over the best single model, confirming\n267\t    the features are redundant across reference architectures. This rules out RC2\n268\t    concerns as irrelevant — the problem is not architecture-specific artifacts but\n269\t    rather the fundamental convergence of generation entropy.\n270\t\n271\t    '\n272\t  tables:\n273\t  - name: 'Table 6: Reference Model Comparison (Leave-One-Generator-Out)'\n274\t    columns:\n275\t    - Reference_Model\n276\t    - LOO_Macro_F1\n277\t    - Ref_Only_F1\n278\t    - Surface_Only_F1\n279\t    rows:\n280\t    - - Pythia-410M\n281\t      - 0.48\n282\t      - 0.47\n283\t      - 0.82\n284\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n285\t      - 0.45\n286\t      - 0.43\n287\t      - 0.82\n288\t    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n289\t      - 0.44\n290\t      - 0.41\n291\t      - 0.82\n292\t    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n293\t      - 0.5\n294\t      - 0.49\n295\t      - 0.82\n296\t  figures_description:\n297\t  - 'Fig 4: Entropy-mean distributions per depth, faceted by reference model. All\n298\t    three reference models show nearly identical overlapping distributions across\n299\t    depths — the convergence phenomenon is reference-model-invariant.'\n300\t  analysis: 'Reference model choice is irrelevant because the underlying signal is\n301\t    absent. Surface features remain constant at 0.82 regardless of reference model\n302\t    (they don''t use it), confirming surface features as the sole discriminative mechanism.\n303\t    The experiment conclusively addresses RC2 but in a way that undermines the paper:\n304\t    the signal isn''t architecture-specific because there is no reference-model signal\n305\t    to be architecture- specific about.\n306\t\n307\t    '\n308\t- name: downstream_utility\n309\t  plan_id: plan_004\n310\t  description: 'Fine-tune Pythia-410M on depth-stratified data subsets. Measure perplexity\n311\t    degradation as function of training data depth. Compare depth-aware filtering\n312\t    vs binary baselines (FastDetectGPT, LD-Score threshold).\n313\t\n314\t    '\n315\t  expected_results:\n316\t    depth_quality_spearman_rho: 0.19\n317\t    depth_quality_spearman_p: 0.04\n318\t    ppl_gen0_only: 18.2\n319\t    ppl_gen1_mixed: 19.1\n320\t    ppl_gen2plus_mixed: 19.8\n321\t    ppl_unfiltered: 19.5\n322\t    depth_filter_top1_ppl: 18.5\n323\t    binary_filter_fastdetect_ppl: 18.4\n324\t    binary_filter_ldscore_ppl: 18.6\n325\t    depth_filter_vs_binary_perf_gap_pct: -0.3\n326\t    depth_filter_data_retained_pct: 38\n327\t    binary_filter_data_retained_pct: 42\n328\t  result_rationale: 'A weak positive correlation (ρ=0.19) exists between predicted\n329\t    depth and quality degradation, but it is barely significant and practically meaningless.\n330\t    The perplexity difference between gen-0-only and gen-2+-mixed training is modest\n331\t    (~1.6 points) because vocabulary impoverishment in synthetic text creates a noisy\n332\t    but not catastrophic training signal — the model still learns reasonable language\n333\t    statistics from synthetic text, just with slightly depleted rare-word coverage.\n334\t    Depth-aware filtering performs equivalently to binary baselines (-0.3% gap, within\n335\t    noise) because the depth classifier''s cross-generator errors (from plan_002)\n336\t    introduce enough noise to eliminate any advantage over simple binary filtering.\n337\t    FastDetectGPT actually edges out depth-aware filtering by 0.1 ppl because it makes\n338\t    fewer classification errors on the binary task than our system makes on the harder\n339\t    ordinal task. Claim 3 effectively fails — depth-aware filtering provides no practical\n340\t    advantage over binary baselines.\n341\t\n342\t    '\n343\t  tables:\n344\t  - name: 'Table 7: Downstream Fine-tuning Perplexity'\n345\t    columns:\n346\t    - Training_Data\n347\t    - Perplexity\n348\t    - Data_Size_pct\n349\t    rows:\n350\t    - - gen-0 only\n351\t      - 18.2\n352\t      - 33\n353\t    - - gen-0 + gen-1\n354\t      - 18.8\n355\t      - 67\n356\t    - - all depths unfiltered\n357\t      - 19.5\n358\t      - 100\n359\t    - - depth-aware filter (≤gen-1)\n360\t      - 18.5\n361\t      - 38\n362\t    - - FastDetectGPT binary filter\n363\t      - 18.4\n364\t      - 42\n365\t    - - LD-Score binary filter\n366\t      - 18.6\n367\t      - 40\n368\t  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Quality Correlation'\n369\t    columns:\n370\t    - Metric\n371\t    - Value\n372\t    rows:\n373\t    - - Spearman ρ (depth vs ppl_degradation)\n374\t      - 0.19\n375\t    - - p-value\n376\t      - 0.04\n377\t    - - depth_filter vs best_binary gap\n378\t      - -0.3%\n379\t  figures_description:\n380\t  - 'Fig 5a: Scatter plot of predicted depth vs perplexity degradation. Weak positive\n381\t    trend with large variance. R² ≈ 0.04. Points heavily overlapping at gen-1/gen-2+\n382\t    boundary.'\n383\t  - 'Fig 5b: Bar chart comparing filtering strategies. Error bars overlap completely\n384\t    between depth-aware and binary baselines. The differences are not practically\n385\t    significant.'\n386\t  analysis: 'Claim 3 fails. While a statistically significant correlation exists (p=0.04),\n387\t    the effect size is negligible (ρ=0.19) and depth-aware filtering underperforms\n388\t    binary baselines by 0.3%. The ordinal depth information adds no practical value\n389\t    over binary classification for data curation. This is the expected consequence\n390\t    of plan_001 and plan_002 results: if depth estimation relies on generator-specific\n391\t    surface features and cross-generator transfer is poor, the depth predictions are\n392\t    too noisy to provide downstream utility beyond what binary detection already offers.\n393\t\n394\t    '\n395\t- name: prompt_type_comparison\n396\t  plan_id: plan_005\n397\t  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n398\t    1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 3 prompt types, 5 depths each.\n399\t\n400\t    '\n401\t  expected_results:\n402\t    continuation_within_gen_f1: 0.82\n403\t    elaboration_within_gen_f1: 0.78\n404\t    inspired_rewriting_within_gen_f1: 0.69\n405\t    continuation_cross_prompt_f1: 0.61\n406\t    elaboration_cross_prompt_f1: 0.58\n407\t    inspired_rewriting_cross_prompt_f1: 0.52\n408\t    convergence_speed_continuation_depth: 2\n409\t    convergence_speed_elaboration_depth: 2\n410\t    convergence_speed_rewriting_depth: 1\n411\t  result_rationale: 'Continuation chains show the highest within-generator F1 (0.82)\n412\t    because they preserve the most topic continuity, making vocabulary depletion the\n413\t    dominant signal. Elaboration is slightly lower (0.78) due to topic drift introducing\n414\t    vocabulary variance that partially masks depletion. Inspired rewriting shows the\n415\t    weakest signal (0.69) because each generation step introduces substantially new\n416\t    vocabulary, partially resetting the depletion signal. Cross-prompt transfer is\n417\t    poor across the board because each prompt type induces a different vocabulary\n418\t    depletion rate — the classifier learns prompt- type-specific thresholds. Convergence\n419\t    analysis shows all three types stabilize by depth 2 (rewriting by depth 1), supporting\n420\t    H₁: even non-paraphrastic chains converge rapidly. The rewriting chains converge\n421\t    fastest because the larger semantic jumps quickly exhaust the generator''s preferred\n422\t    vocabulary, reaching a \"vocabulary floor\" by gen-1.\n423\t\n424\t    '\n425\t  tables:\n426\t  - name: 'Table 9: Prompt Type Comparison'\n427\t    columns:\n428\t    - Prompt_Type\n429\t    - Within_Gen_F1\n430\t    - Cross_Prompt_F1\n431\t    - Convergence_Depth\n432\t    rows:\n433\t    - - continuation\n434\t      - 0.82\n435\t      - 0.61\n436\t      - 2\n437\t    - - elaboration\n438\t      - 0.78\n439\t      - 0.58\n440\t      - 2\n441\t    - - inspired_rewriting\n442\t      - 0.69\n443\t      - 0.52\n444\t      - 1\n445\t  figures_description:\n446\t  - 'Fig 6a: Per-depth hapax_ratio trajectories for each prompt type. All three converge\n447\t    but at different rates and to different asymptotes. Rewriting flattens by gen-1;\n448\t    continuation shows a gradual decline through gen-2 then plateaus.'\n449\t  - 'Fig 6b: Feature distribution overlap (KL divergence between consecutive depths)\n450\t    per prompt type. All types show rapid KL collapse, with gen-2→gen-3 KL < 0.05\n451\t    nats for all types.'\n452\t  analysis: 'All prompt types show rapid convergence, supporting H₁. The depth signal\n453\t    ceiling is approximately 2 effective classes (gen-0 vs synthetic) for rewriting\n454\t    and 3 classes (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) for continuation — but only within-generator.\n455\t    The prompt type comparison reveals an additional confound: not only is the signal\n456\t    generator-specific, it is prompt-type-specific. This further narrows the practical\n457\t    applicability of depth estimation.\n458\t\n459\t    '\n460\t- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n461\t  plan_id: plan_006\n462\t  description: 'Construct parallel paraphrasing chains for direct comparison with\n463\t    continuation chains. Test attractor convergence theory — H₁ vs H₂.\n464\t\n465\t    '\n466\t  expected_results:\n467\t    paraphrase_within_gen_f1: 0.64\n468\t    continuation_within_gen_f1: 0.82\n469\t    paraphrase_convergence_depth: 1\n470\t    continuation_convergence_depth: 2\n471\t    paraphrase_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.41\n472\t    continuation_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.58\n473\t    kl_depth2_vs_depth3_paraphrase: 0.008\n474\t    kl_depth2_vs_depth3_continuation: 0.031\n475\t  result_rationale: 'Paraphrasing chains converge faster than continuation chains\n476\t    (by depth 1 vs depth 2), consistent with attractor cycle theory from ACL 2025.\n477\t    Paraphrasing F1 is lower (0.64) because the semantic-preservation constraint limits\n478\t    vocabulary variation — paraphrases reuse similar word types, so hapax_ratio depletion\n479\t    is less pronounced. But crucially, continuation chains also converge by depth\n480\t    2, meaning non-paraphrastic generation does NOT produce the unbounded divergence\n481\t    hypothesized in H₂. Both chain types reach near-stationary distributions within\n482\t    2-3 steps. The KL divergence between depth-2 and depth-3 is negligible for both\n483\t    types (<0.04 nats). This is the key finding for the worst-case narrative: H₁ (convergence)\n484\t    holds even for non-paraphrastic chains, limiting depth signal to a maximum of\n485\t    ~2-3 discriminable classes and undermining the premise that non-paraphrastic chains\n486\t    produce monotonic divergence.\n487\t\n488\t    '\n489\t  tables:\n490\t  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Non-Paraphrastic Chains'\n491\t    columns:\n492\t    - Chain_Type\n493\t    - Macro_F1_3class\n494\t    - Gen1_vs_Gen2plus_F1\n495\t    - Convergence_Depth\n496\t    - KL_d2_d3\n497\t    rows:\n498\t    - - paraphrasing\n499\t      - 0.64\n500\t      - 0.41\n501\t      - 1\n502\t      - 0.008\n503\t    - - continuation\n504\t      - 0.82\n505\t      - 0.58\n506\t      - 2\n507\t      - 0.031\n508\t    - - elaboration\n509\t      - 0.78\n510\t      - 0.52\n511\t      - 2\n512\t      - 0.024\n513\t    - - inspired_rewriting\n514\t      - 0.69\n515\t      - 0.44\n516\t      - 1\n517\t      - 0.012\n518\t  figures_description:\n519\t  - 'Fig 7: Feature trajectory plots (hapax_ratio, entropy_mean) across depths 0-4,\n520\t    paraphrastic vs continuation. Both converge but continuation has a slightly longer\n521\t    transient. Shaded regions show ±1σ across seeds. Both curves flatten by depth\n522\t    2-3.'\n523\t  analysis: 'H₁ is confirmed: non-paraphrastic chains also converge, just 1 step slower\n524\t    than paraphrastic chains. This extends the ACL 2025 attractor theory to non-paraphrastic\n525\t    settings — arguably the most publishable finding in the worst case. The convergence\n526\t    is driven by autoregressive sampling from a fixed model concentrating probability\n527\t    mass on preferred tokens regardless of prompt type, creating a universal vocabulary\n528\t    attractor. Depth signal is fundamentally bounded at ~2-3 classes.\n529\t\n530\t    '\n531\t- name: cross_model_chains\n532\t  plan_id: plan_007\n533\t  description: 'Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step) vs single-model\n534\t    chains. 5 generators randomly assigned per step. 1000 seeds × 5 depths.\n535\t\n536\t    '\n537\t  expected_results:\n538\t    single_model_within_gen_f1: 0.81\n539\t    cross_model_chain_f1: 0.42\n540\t    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.65\n541\t    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.32\n542\t    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.35\n543\t    cross_model_binary_gen0_vs_rest_f1: 0.67\n544\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are devastating for depth estimation (0.42\n545\t    macro-F1). When different generators produce each depth step, the vocabulary statistics\n546\t    at any given depth reflect the current-step generator''s preferences more than\n547\t    the cumulative depth of the chain. A text generated by Qwen at depth-2 in a chain\n548\t    may have higher hapax_ratio than text generated by Pythia-1B at depth-1, because\n549\t    Qwen has a richer base vocabulary. The generator-switching effectively randomizes\n550\t    the surface features that the classifier depends on. Gen-0 identification survives\n551\t    partially (0.65) because human text is still distinct from any generator, but\n552\t    gen-1/gen-2+ classification is near-chance. This is the most realistic contamination\n553\t    scenario and the most damning result for practical applicability.\n554\t\n555\t    '\n556\t  tables:\n557\t  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chains'\n558\t    columns:\n559\t    - Setting\n560\t    - Macro_F1\n561\t    - Gen0_F1\n562\t    - Gen1_F1\n563\t    - Gen2plus_F1\n564\t    rows:\n565\t    - - single_model_chains\n566\t      - 0.81\n567\t      - 0.94\n568\t      - 0.75\n569\t      - 0.77\n570\t    - - cross_model_chains\n571\t      - 0.42\n572\t      - 0.65\n573\t      - 0.32\n574\t      - 0.35\n575\t    - - chance\n576\t      - 0.33\n577\t      - 0.33\n578\t      - 0.33\n579\t      - 0.33\n580\t  figures_description:\n581\t  - 'Fig 8: Paired bar chart showing collapse from single-model to cross-model setting.\n582\t    Gen-1 and gen-2+ bars drop to near-chance. Annotation: ''generator identity dominates\n583\t    depth signal.'''\n584\t  analysis: 'The most ecologically valid setting (cross-model chains) produces near-chance\n585\t    depth estimation. This confirms that the MVP result (0.84) was an artifact of\n586\t    single-model evaluation: the classifier learned Pythia-2.8B''s specific vocabulary\n587\t    depletion curve, not a universal depth signature. In realistic web contamination\n588\t    scenarios with diverse generators, depth estimation beyond binary (human vs synthetic)\n589\t    is not feasible with current features.\n590\t\n591\t    '\n592\t- name: multi_domain_seeds\n593\t  plan_id: plan_008\n594\t  description: 'Expand to 4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC),\n595\t    creative writing (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 depths, Pythia-2.8B\n596\t    generator.\n597\t\n598\t    '\n599\t  expected_results:\n600\t    wikipedia_within_domain_f1: 0.83\n601\t    news_within_domain_f1: 0.8\n602\t    scientific_within_domain_f1: 0.76\n603\t    creative_within_domain_f1: 0.72\n604\t    cross_domain_avg_f1: 0.59\n605\t    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.74\n606\t    scientific_hapax_ratio_gen0_median: 0.52\n607\t    creative_hapax_ratio_gen0_median: 0.38\n608\t  result_rationale: 'Within-domain performance is reasonable but varies by domain.\n609\t    Wikipedia/news are easiest because their seed texts have high hapax_ratio (specialized\n610\t    proper nouns, rare terms) that generation rapidly depletes. Scientific text starts\n611\t    with high rare-word density (technical terms) but the generator sometimes preserves\n612\t    or regenerates these, creating noise. Creative writing is hardest because its\n613\t    base vocabulary is less specialized — the hapax_ratio gap between gen-0 and gen-1\n614\t    is smaller. Cross-domain transfer drops to 0.59 because each domain has different\n615\t    baseline vocabulary statistics: scientific gen-0 text (hapax_ratio ~0.52) overlaps\n616\t    with creative gen-0 (~0.38), confounding domain and depth. This adds another axis\n617\t    of non-generalization beyond generator and prompt type.\n618\t\n619\t    '\n620\t  tables:\n621\t  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Results'\n622\t    columns:\n623\t    - Domain\n624\t    - Within_Domain_F1\n625\t    - Gen0_hapax_median\n626\t    - Gen2plus_hapax_median\n627\t    - Δ_hapax\n628\t    rows:\n629\t    - - Wikipedia\n630\t      - 0.83\n631\t      - 0.47\n632\t      - 0.26\n633\t      - 0.21\n634\t    - - News\n635\t      - 0.8\n636\t      - 0.44\n637\t      - 0.27\n638\t      - 0.17\n639\t    - - Scientific\n640\t      - 0.76\n641\t      - 0.52\n642\t      - 0.35\n643\t      - 0.17\n644\t    - - Creative\n645\t      - 0.72\n646\t      - 0.38\n647\t      - 0.28\n648\t      - 0.1\n649\t    - - Cross-domain avg\n650\t      - 0.59\n651\t      - '-'\n652\t      - '-'\n653\t      - '-'\n654\t  figures_description:\n655\t  - 'Fig 9: Per-domain hapax_ratio trajectories across depths. All four domains show\n656\t    monotonic decrease but with different baselines and slopes. Scientific and creative\n657\t    curves overlap substantially.'\n658\t  analysis: 'Domain is yet another confound. The depth signal is domain-specific because\n659\t    the magnitude of vocabulary depletion depends on the seed text''s lexical richness.\n660\t    Combined with generator-specificity and prompt-type-specificity, this creates\n661\t    a three-way interaction that makes general-purpose depth estimation impractical.\n662\t    A classifier trained on Wikipedia+news will overestimate depth for creative writing\n663\t    (lower base hapax) and underestimate it for scientific text (higher base hapax).\n664\t\n665\t    '\n666\t- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n667\t  plan_id: plan_009\n668\t  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on non-paraphrastic\n669\t    continuation chains. Tests if more than 3-class depth signal exists.\n670\t\n671\t    '\n672\t  expected_results:\n673\t    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.52\n674\t    ordinal_5class_gen0_f1: 0.91\n675\t    ordinal_5class_gen1_f1: 0.58\n676\t    ordinal_5class_gen2_f1: 0.38\n677\t    ordinal_5class_gen3_f1: 0.31\n678\t    ordinal_5class_gen4_f1: 0.33\n679\t    ordinal_3class_macro_f1: 0.82\n680\t    adjacent_class_confusion_rate: 0.44\n681\t    mae_ordinal: 0.72\n682\t  result_rationale: '5-class performance drops sharply to 0.52 macro-F1 (from 0.82\n683\t    for 3-class), with gen-2 through gen-4 collapsing into near-chance mutual confusion.\n684\t    This is the direct consequence of H₁ convergence: by depth 2, the feature distributions\n685\t    have already converged, so gen-2/gen-3/gen-4 are statistically indistinguishable.\n686\t    The confusion is concentrated in adjacent classes (44% of errors are off-by-one),\n687\t    consistent with a smooth convergence rather than discrete depth categories. Gen-0\n688\t    remains easily identified (0.91) and gen-1 partially (0.58), but beyond gen-1\n689\t    the signal is gone. This confirms the practical ceiling is 2-3 discriminable depth\n690\t    classes, not finer- grained ordinal estimation.\n691\t\n692\t    '\n693\t  tables:\n694\t  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Classification'\n695\t    columns:\n696\t    - Class\n697\t    - Precision\n698\t    - Recall\n699\t    - F1\n700\t    rows:\n701\t    - - gen-0\n702\t      - 0.93\n703\t      - 0.9\n704\t      - 0.91\n705\t    - - gen-1\n706\t      - 0.54\n707\t      - 0.62\n708\t      - 0.58\n709\t    - - gen-2\n710\t      - 0.36\n711\t      - 0.4\n712\t      - 0.38\n713\t    - - gen-3\n714\t      - 0.29\n715\t      - 0.33\n716\t      - 0.31\n717\t    - - gen-4\n718\t      - 0.31\n719\t      - 0.35\n720\t      - 0.33\n721\t  - name: 'Table 14: 5-Class Confusion Matrix'\n722\t    columns:\n723\t    - Predicted→\n724\t    - gen-0\n725\t    - gen-1\n726\t    - gen-2\n727\t    - gen-3\n728\t    - gen-4\n729\t    rows:\n730\t    - - gen-0\n731\t      - 0.9\n732\t      - 0.07\n733\t      - 0.02\n734\t      - 0.01\n735\t      - 0.0\n736\t    - - gen-1\n737\t      - 0.09\n738\t      - 0.62\n739\t      - 0.18\n740\t      - 0.07\n741\t      - 0.04\n742\t    - - gen-2\n743\t      - 0.03\n744\t      - 0.21\n745\t      - 0.4\n746\t      - 0.22\n747\t      - 0.14\n748\t    - - gen-3\n749\t      - 0.02\n750\t      - 0.1\n751\t      - 0.24\n752\t      - 0.33\n753\t      - 0.31\n754\t    - - gen-4\n755\t      - 0.01\n756\t      - 0.07\n757\t      - 0.16\n758\t      - 0.28\n759\t      - 0.48\n760\t  figures_description:\n761\t  - 'Fig 10: Per-class F1 as function of number of ordinal classes (2, 3, 4, 5). Sharp\n762\t    elbow at 3 classes — adding a 4th or 5th class produces near-chance F1 for the\n763\t    new classes without improving existing ones.'\n764\t  analysis: 'Finer-grained estimation is not feasible. The depth signal has a hard\n765\t    ceiling at ~3 discriminable classes, consistent with convergence by depth 2. The\n766\t    confusion matrix shows a clear diagonal-plus-adjacent pattern typical of ordinal\n767\t    features with limited dynamic range. Gen-3 and gen-4 are essentially exchangeable.\n768\t    This further limits the practical scope of depth estimation.\n769\t\n770\t    '\n771\t- name: human_edit_robustness\n772\t  plan_id: plan_010\n773\t  description: 'Robustness to simulated human edits. (A) Random word replacement at\n774\t    5%, 10%, 20%. (B) LLM-simulated editing (light rephrasing of random sentences).\n775\t\n776\t    '\n777\t  expected_results:\n778\t    baseline_no_edit_f1: 0.82\n779\t    random_replace_5pct_f1: 0.77\n780\t    random_replace_10pct_f1: 0.7\n781\t    random_replace_20pct_f1: 0.58\n782\t    llm_edit_light_f1: 0.63\n783\t    llm_edit_light_gen0_f1: 0.82\n784\t    llm_edit_light_gen1_f1: 0.51\n785\t    llm_edit_light_gen2plus_f1: 0.48\n786\t  result_rationale: 'Editing disrupts the surface features that carry all the signal.\n787\t    Even 5% random word replacement drops F1 by 0.05 because replaced words tend to\n788\t    be common words replaced with less-common alternatives, artificially inflating\n789\t    hapax_ratio and masking vocabulary depletion. At 20%, the editing noise dominates\n790\t    the depth signal. LLM- simulated editing (light rephrasing) is particularly damaging\n791\t    (0.63) because the editing LLM introduces its own vocabulary preferences, adding\n792\t    a second generator''s fingerprint on top of the original. Since the classifier\n793\t    relies on vocabulary statistics, any post-hoc vocabulary injection confounds depth\n794\t    estimation. This confirms the fragility of surface-feature-based depth estimation\n795\t    in realistic scenarios where synthetic text is edited before publication.\n796\t\n797\t    '\n798\t  tables:\n799\t  - name: 'Table 15: Robustness to Human Edits'\n800\t    columns:\n801\t    - Edit_Type\n802\t    - Edit_Rate\n803\t    - Macro_F1\n804\t    - Δ_vs_Baseline\n805\t    rows:\n806\t    - - none\n807\t      - 0%\n808\t      - 0.82\n809\t      - '-'\n810\t    - - random_replace\n811\t      - 5%\n812\t      - 0.77\n813\t      - -0.05\n814\t    - - random_replace\n815\t      - 10%\n816\t      - 0.7\n817\t      - -0.12\n818\t    - - random_replace\n819\t      - 20%\n820\t      - 0.58\n821\t      - -0.24\n822\t    - - llm_simulated\n823\t      - light\n824\t      - 0.63\n825\t      - -0.19\n826\t  figures_description:\n827\t  - 'Fig 11: F1 degradation curve as function of edit rate. Approximately linear decline.\n828\t    Dashed line marks the point where depth estimation drops below binary baseline\n829\t    performance (~12% edit rate).'\n830\t  analysis: 'Surface features are fragile under editing. The crossover point where\n831\t    depth estimation becomes worse than binary detection occurs at approximately 12%\n832\t    word replacement rate. Real-world synthetic text is routinely edited at this level\n833\t    or above, further limiting practical applicability.\n834\t\n835\t    '\n836\t- name: ld_score_feature\n837\t  plan_id: plan_011\n838\t  description: 'Add LD-Score (character-distribution signature) as additional feature.\n839\t    Tests whether character-level features provide complementary depth signal.\n840\t\n841\t    '\n842\t  expected_results:\n843\t    surface_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n844\t    ld_only_macro_f1: 0.61\n845\t    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n846\t    ld_cross_gen_loo_f1: 0.46\n847\t    ld_feature_importance: 0.05\n848\t  result_rationale: 'LD-Score as a standalone feature performs moderately (0.61) —\n849\t    better than reference- model features (0.47) but worse than surface features (0.82).\n850\t    This is because character-level distributions partially capture vocabulary depletion\n851\t    through character frequency shifts, providing a smoothed proxy for hapax_ratio.\n852\t    However, adding LD-Score to surface features yields negligible improvement (+0.01)\n853\t    because the character-level signal is redundant with word-level surface statistics.\n854\t    Cross-generator transfer with LD-Score (0.46) is no better than without, confirming\n855\t    that character-level features also suffer from generator-specificity. LD-Score\n856\t    does not rescue the fundamental problem.\n857\t\n858\t    '\n859\t  tables:\n860\t  - name: 'Table 16: LD-Score Feature Analysis'\n861\t    columns:\n862\t    - Feature_Set\n863\t    - Macro_F1\n864\t    - LOO_Cross_Gen_F1\n865\t    rows:\n866\t    - - surface_only\n867\t      - 0.82\n868\t      - 0.48\n869\t    - - ld_only\n870\t      - 0.61\n871\t      - 0.46\n872\t    - - surface + ld\n873\t      - 0.83\n874\t      - 0.49\n875\t    - - full + ld\n876\t      - 0.83\n877\t      - 0.5\n878\t  figures_description:\n879\t  - 'Fig S1 (appendix): LD-Score distribution per depth. Moderate separation between\n880\t    gen-0 and gen-2+ but heavy overlap at gen-1, similar to ref-model entropy features.'\n881\t  analysis: 'LD-Score is a character-level echo of word-level vocabulary depletion\n882\t    — correlated but not complementary. It does not provide a new axis of depth information.\n883\t    Including it in the paper is optional; it addresses a potential reviewer question\n884\t    but doesn''t change conclusions.\n885\t\n886\t    '\n887\t- name: end_to_end_classifier\n888\t  plan_id: plan_012\n889\t  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification.\n890\t    Compares learned representations vs handcrafted features.\n891\t\n892\t    '\n893\t  expected_results:\n894\t    e2e_within_gen_macro_f1: 0.87\n895\t    e2e_cross_gen_loo_macro_f1: 0.53\n896\t    e2e_cross_gen_gen0_f1: 0.74\n897\t    e2e_cross_gen_gen1_f1: 0.42\n898\t    e2e_cross_gen_gen2plus_f1: 0.45\n899\t    e2e_vs_handcrafted_within_delta: 0.05\n900\t    e2e_vs_handcrafted_cross_delta: 0.05\n901\t    e2e_attention_analysis_top_tokens:\n902\t    - the\n903\t    - of\n904\t    - is\n905\t    - .\n906\t    - ','\n907\t  result_rationale: 'The end-to-end model achieves marginally higher within-generator\n908\t    F1 (0.87 vs 0.82) by learning soft vocabulary depletion features beyond hapax_ratio\n909\t    and TTR — it implicitly captures the full vocabulary distribution shift through\n910\t    its representations. However, cross-generator transfer shows only modest improvement\n911\t    (0.53 vs 0.48), meaning the learned representations are also predominantly generator-specific.\n912\t    Attention analysis reveals the model focuses on high-frequency function words\n913\t    and punctuation — exactly the tokens whose usage patterns shift under vocabulary\n914\t    impoverishment. The learned model does not discover hidden distributional structure\n915\t    beyond surface statistics; it simply learns a richer encoding of the same surface\n916\t    phenomenon. The 0.05 cross-gen improvement over handcrafted features is the only\n917\t    hint that some generator-invariant signal might exist in deeper representations,\n918\t    but it is not large enough to be practically useful.\n919\t\n920\t    '\n921\t  tables:\n922\t  - name: 'Table 17: End-to-End vs Handcrafted Features'\n923\t    columns:\n924\t    - Model\n925\t    - Within_Gen_F1\n926\t    - Cross_Gen_LOO_F1\n927\t    - Δ_Cross_Gen\n928\t    rows:\n929\t    - - handcrafted (surface + ref)\n930\t      - 0.83\n931\t      - 0.48\n932\t      - '-'\n933\t    - - QLoRA Qwen3.5-0.8B\n934\t      - 0.87\n935\t      - 0.53\n936\t      - 0.05\n937\t    - - handcrafted surface_only\n938\t      - 0.82\n939\t      - 0.48\n940\t      - '-'\n941\t  figures_description:\n942\t  - 'Fig S2 (appendix): Attention heatmaps from fine-tuned model on gen-0 vs gen-2+\n943\t    examples. Model attends heavily to function words and sentence boundaries — consistent\n944\t    with vocabulary impoverishment detection rather than deeper distributional analysis.'\n945\t  - 'Fig S3 (appendix): t-SNE of learned representations. Slight improvement in depth\n946\t    separation vs handcrafted features, but generator clusters still dominate the\n947\t    embedding space.'\n948\t  analysis: 'The end-to-end model confirms the ceiling: even with 0.8B parameters\n949\t    and full sequence access, cross-generator depth estimation barely exceeds chance\n950\t    for gen-1 and gen-2+. The model learns vocabulary impoverishment more efficiently\n951\t    than handcrafted features (+0.05 within-gen) but cannot overcome the fundamental\n952\t    generator-specificity of the signal (+0.05 cross-gen, still at 0.53). This closes\n953\t    the \"maybe a bigger model could find it\" escape hatch — the signal genuinely isn''t\n954\t    there for cross-generator depth estimation.'\n955\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad9f5738-9994-4640-8a26-12b004b49048","uuid":"e7a61368-9cd6-4a81-b24d-16e6c3193aef","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:56:20.737Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T181213/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Abandon \"distributional fingerprint\" as primary contribution.\n  Surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) explain nearly all classifier variance; reference-model\n  entropy statistics add negligible independent signal. Cross-generator generalization\n  collapses because surface vocabulary statistics are generator-specific artifacts,\n  not universal depth markers. Reframe paper as an empirical characterization of recursive\n  generation dynamics: \"We tested H₁ vs H₂ and found that non-paraphrastic chains\n  also converge rapidly in distributional space, with detectable differences confined\n  primarily to vocabulary impoverishment.\" Reposition as negative-but-informative\n  result extending attractor cycle theory to non-paraphrastic settings. New angle:\n  vocabulary impoverishment as a universal but shallow fingerprint — theoretically\n  motivated by generation entropy convergence under autoregressive sampling. Target\n  AAAI as empirical science contribution with clear hypothesis-testing framing and\n  boundary conditions for future work.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: feature_ablation\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Ablation of reference-only, surface-only, and full feature sets on\n    MVP data (Pythia-2.8B generator, Pythia-410M reference, 3-class depth). Ordinal\n    logistic regression + random forest, 5-fold CV.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    surface_only_macro_f1: 0.82\n    surface_only_cv_std: 0.015\n    ref_only_macro_f1: 0.47\n    ref_only_cv_std: 0.032\n    full_macro_f1: 0.83\n    full_cv_std: 0.014\n    ref_minus_surface_delta: -0.35\n    full_minus_surface_delta: 0.01\n    permutation_importance_hapax_ratio: 0.31\n    permutation_importance_ttr: 0.24\n    permutation_importance_entropy_mean: 0.04\n    permutation_importance_entropy_std: 0.03\n    permutation_importance_tail_decay: 0.02\n    ref_features_added_significance_p: 0.18\n  result_rationale: 'Surface features alone recover 97.6% of full-model performance\n    (0.82 vs 0.84 MVP). hapax_ratio and TTR dominate because recursive generation\n    progressively depletes rare vocabulary — a mechanical consequence of autoregressive\n    sampling from a fixed distribution. Reference-model entropy statistics show near-chance\n    3-class performance (0.47) because the entropy distribution under the reference\n    model converges rapidly across generation depths: by gen-1, the token-level surprise\n    distribution is already close to its stationary form. Adding reference features\n    to surface features yields only +0.01 F1 (not statistically significant, p=0.18\n    on paired bootstrap), confirming that entropy_mean/std/min/max carry redundant\n    information already captured by vocabulary depletion proxies. The \"distributional\n    fingerprint\" framing collapses — what we detect is vocabulary impoverishment,\n    not a deeper distributional shift.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 2: Feature Ablation Results (3-class, MVP data)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature_Set\n    - Macro_F1\n    - Gen0_F1\n    - Gen1_F1\n    - Gen2plus_F1\n    - CV_Std\n    rows:\n    - - surface_only\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.015\n    - - ref_only\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.032\n    - - full\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.014\n    - - chance\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - '-'\n  - name: 'Table 3: Permutation Feature Importance (Full Model)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Importance\n    - Std\n    rows:\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.04\n    - - ttr\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.03\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.02\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.01\n    - - entropy_min\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.01\n    - - entropy_max\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.01\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.01\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 2a: Violin plot of per-depth feature distributions. hapax_ratio shows clear\n    monotonic decrease (gen-0 median ~0.42, gen-1 ~0.31, gen-2+ ~0.27). entropy_mean\n    shows overlapping distributions with only gen-0 slightly separated.'\n  - 'Fig 2b: ROC curves for each feature set. Surface-only and full curves nearly\n    overlap (AUC 0.91 vs 0.92). Ref-only curve barely above diagonal for gen-1 vs\n    gen-2+ (AUC 0.56).'\n  - 'Fig 2c: Confusion matrices side-by-side. Ref-only model conflates gen-1 and gen-2+\n    heavily (off-diagonal mass ~55%). Surface-only cleanly separates gen-0 but confuses\n    gen-1/gen-2+ at ~25% rate.'\n  analysis: 'Claim 2 is falsified. The ablation decisively shows that reference-model\n    features carry no independent signal. The full model''s marginal +0.01 over surface-only\n    is within noise. This result is consistent with the hypothesis that autoregressive\n    generation entropy converges to a near-stationary distribution by gen-1, while\n    vocabulary depletion (a surface-level phenomenon) continues to accumulate. The\n    practical implication: a $0 feature (hapax_ratio) matches the expensive reference-model\n    inference pipeline.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: '5 generators (Pythia-2.8B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3.5-2B, Qwen3.5-0.8B, Pythia-1B).\n    1000 seeds × 5 generators × 5 depths = 25K chains. Leave-one-generator-out CV\n    for cross-generator generalization. 3-class depth classification.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    within_generator_avg_macro_f1: 0.81\n    leave_one_out_avg_macro_f1: 0.48\n    leave_one_out_std: 0.07\n    leave_one_out_gen0_f1: 0.68\n    leave_one_out_gen1_f1: 0.38\n    leave_one_out_gen2plus_f1: 0.42\n    loo_held_out_pythia28b_f1: 0.52\n    loo_held_out_olmo_f1: 0.44\n    loo_held_out_qwen2b_f1: 0.43\n    loo_held_out_qwen08b_f1: 0.5\n    loo_held_out_pythia1b_f1: 0.53\n    gen1_vs_gen2plus_binary_loo_f1: 0.54\n    pooled_all_generators_macro_f1: 0.62\n  result_rationale: 'Within-generator performance remains strong (~0.81) because each\n    generator has a characteristic vocabulary depletion rate that surface features\n    learn. But leave-one-out drops catastrophically to 0.48 because the absolute levels\n    of hapax_ratio and TTR are generator-specific: Qwen models produce systematically\n    different type-token ratios than Pythia models at every depth. The classifier\n    learns generator-specific thresholds, not universal depth signatures. Gen-0 identification\n    is partially salvaged (0.68) because human-written text has a consistently distinct\n    surface profile regardless of which generator produces the synthetic continuations.\n    Gen-1 vs gen-2+ discrimination is near chance (0.38/0.42) in the cross-generator\n    setting because the vocabulary depletion curves of different generators at depth\n    1 overlap with other generators at depth 2+. Pythia-family held-out sets perform\n    slightly better (0.52-0.53) due to Pythia-1B sharing vocabulary statistics with\n    Pythia-2.8B in the training fold. Cross- family transfer (Qwen held out) is worst\n    (0.43-0.44). This confirms that surface features are generator-specific, not universal\n    depth markers.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Cross-Generator Generalization (Leave-One-Out)'\n    columns:\n    - Held_Out_Generator\n    - Macro_F1\n    - Gen0_F1\n    - Gen1_F1\n    - Gen2plus_F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.45\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.38\n    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.39\n    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.43\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - 0.53\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.46\n    - - '**Average**'\n      - '**0.48**'\n      - '**0.68**'\n      - '**0.38**'\n      - '**0.42**'\n  - name: 'Table 5: Within- vs Cross-Generator Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Setting\n    - Macro_F1\n    - Δ_vs_Within\n    rows:\n    - - within_generator_avg\n      - 0.81\n      - '-'\n    - - leave_one_out_avg\n      - 0.48\n      - -0.33\n    - - pooled_all_generators\n      - 0.62\n      - -0.19\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 3a: Per-generator hapax_ratio distributions at each depth. Each generator\n    occupies a distinct band — Qwen3.5-2B gen-0 median overlaps with Pythia-2.8B gen-1\n    median, explaining cross-generator confusion.'\n  - 'Fig 3b: Heatmap of leave-one-out F1 (held-out generator × depth class). Clear\n    gradient: gen-0 row is yellow (decent), gen-1 and gen-2+ rows are dark red (near-chance).'\n  - 'Fig 3c: t-SNE of feature space colored by generator. Points cluster by generator\n    first, depth second — generator identity dominates the feature space geometry.'\n  analysis: 'Claim 1 fails in its generalization requirement. Cross-generator macro-F1\n    of 0.48 is barely above chance (0.33). The fundamental problem: surface vocabulary\n    statistics are generator-specific calibration artifacts. A model that learns \"hapax_ratio\n    < 0.30 means gen-2+\" from Pythia data will misclassify Qwen gen-0 text (which\n    may have hapax_ratio ~0.35 vs Pythia gen-0 ~0.45). Without independent reference-model\n    signal (which plan_001 showed is absent), there is no generator-invariant depth\n    feature. The pooled setting (0.62) is inflated by within-generator signal bleeding\n    through the non-held-out generators in training.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_family_reference_model\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Test OLMo-2-1B and Qwen3.5-0.8B as reference models (replacing Pythia-410M)\n    for feature extraction. Full cross of 3 reference models × 5 generators.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    pythia_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.48\n    olmo_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.45\n    qwen_ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.44\n    best_ref_model: pythia_410m\n    ref_model_variance_std: 0.02\n    ensemble_3ref_cross_gen_macro_f1: 0.5\n    ref_only_olmo_macro_f1: 0.43\n    ref_only_qwen_macro_f1: 0.41\n  result_rationale: 'Changing the reference model does not rescue cross-generator\n    performance because the bottleneck is not reference model choice — it is the absence\n    of depth-discriminative signal in the entropy statistics themselves. All three\n    reference models produce similarly uninformative entropy distributions because\n    the phenomenon is fundamental: autoregressive generation entropy converges to\n    near-stationary levels by gen-1 regardless of which model measures it. The slight\n    advantage of Pythia-410M (0.48 vs 0.44-0.45) is a minor architectural compatibility\n    effect with the Pythia generators in the training set, not genuine signal. Ensembling\n    three reference models yields only +0.02 over the best single model, confirming\n    the features are redundant across reference architectures. This rules out RC2\n    concerns as irrelevant — the problem is not architecture-specific artifacts but\n    rather the fundamental convergence of generation entropy.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 6: Reference Model Comparison (Leave-One-Generator-Out)'\n    columns:\n    - Reference_Model\n    - LOO_Macro_F1\n    - Ref_Only_F1\n    - Surface_Only_F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-410M\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.82\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - 0.45\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.82\n    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.82\n    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.49\n      - 0.82\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 4: Entropy-mean distributions per depth, faceted by reference model. All\n    three reference models show nearly identical overlapping distributions across\n    depths — the convergence phenomenon is reference-model-invariant.'\n  analysis: 'Reference model choice is irrelevant because the underlying signal is\n    absent. Surface features remain constant at 0.82 regardless of reference model\n    (they don''t use it), confirming surface features as the sole discriminative mechanism.\n    The experiment conclusively addresses RC2 but in a way that undermines the paper:\n    the signal isn''t architecture-specific because there is no reference-model signal\n    to be architecture- specific about.\n\n    '\n- name: downstream_utility\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Fine-tune Pythia-410M on depth-stratified data subsets. Measure perplexity\n    degradation as function of training data depth. Compare depth-aware filtering\n    vs binary baselines (FastDetectGPT, LD-Score threshold).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    depth_quality_spearman_rho: 0.19\n    depth_quality_spearman_p: 0.04\n    ppl_gen0_only: 18.2\n    ppl_gen1_mixed: 19.1\n    ppl_gen2plus_mixed: 19.8\n    ppl_unfiltered: 19.5\n    depth_filter_top1_ppl: 18.5\n    binary_filter_fastdetect_ppl: 18.4\n    binary_filter_ldscore_ppl: 18.6\n    depth_filter_vs_binary_perf_gap_pct: -0.3\n    depth_filter_data_retained_pct: 38\n    binary_filter_data_retained_pct: 42\n  result_rationale: 'A weak positive correlation (ρ=0.19) exists between predicted\n    depth and quality degradation, but it is barely significant and practically meaningless.\n    The perplexity difference between gen-0-only and gen-2+-mixed training is modest\n    (~1.6 points) because vocabulary impoverishment in synthetic text creates a noisy\n    but not catastrophic training signal — the model still learns reasonable language\n    statistics from synthetic text, just with slightly depleted rare-word coverage.\n    Depth-aware filtering performs equivalently to binary baselines (-0.3% gap, within\n    noise) because the depth classifier''s cross-generator errors (from plan_002)\n    introduce enough noise to eliminate any advantage over simple binary filtering.\n    FastDetectGPT actually edges out depth-aware filtering by 0.1 ppl because it makes\n    fewer classification errors on the binary task than our system makes on the harder\n    ordinal task. Claim 3 effectively fails — depth-aware filtering provides no practical\n    advantage over binary baselines.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Downstream Fine-tuning Perplexity'\n    columns:\n    - Training_Data\n    - Perplexity\n    - Data_Size_pct\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0 only\n      - 18.2\n      - 33\n    - - gen-0 + gen-1\n      - 18.8\n      - 67\n    - - all depths unfiltered\n      - 19.5\n      - 100\n    - - depth-aware filter (≤gen-1)\n      - 18.5\n      - 38\n    - - FastDetectGPT binary filter\n      - 18.4\n      - 42\n    - - LD-Score binary filter\n      - 18.6\n      - 40\n  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Quality Correlation'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    rows:\n    - - Spearman ρ (depth vs ppl_degradation)\n      - 0.19\n    - - p-value\n      - 0.04\n    - - depth_filter vs best_binary gap\n      - -0.3%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 5a: Scatter plot of predicted depth vs perplexity degradation. Weak positive\n    trend with large variance. R² ≈ 0.04. Points heavily overlapping at gen-1/gen-2+\n    boundary.'\n  - 'Fig 5b: Bar chart comparing filtering strategies. Error bars overlap completely\n    between depth-aware and binary baselines. The differences are not practically\n    significant.'\n  analysis: 'Claim 3 fails. While a statistically significant correlation exists (p=0.04),\n    the effect size is negligible (ρ=0.19) and depth-aware filtering underperforms\n    binary baselines by 0.3%. The ordinal depth information adds no practical value\n    over binary classification for data curation. This is the expected consequence\n    of plan_001 and plan_002 results: if depth estimation relies on generator-specific\n    surface features and cross-generator transfer is poor, the depth predictions are\n    too noisy to provide downstream utility beyond what binary detection already offers.\n\n    '\n- name: prompt_type_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n    1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B, 3 prompt types, 5 depths each.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    continuation_within_gen_f1: 0.82\n    elaboration_within_gen_f1: 0.78\n    inspired_rewriting_within_gen_f1: 0.69\n    continuation_cross_prompt_f1: 0.61\n    elaboration_cross_prompt_f1: 0.58\n    inspired_rewriting_cross_prompt_f1: 0.52\n    convergence_speed_continuation_depth: 2\n    convergence_speed_elaboration_depth: 2\n    convergence_speed_rewriting_depth: 1\n  result_rationale: 'Continuation chains show the highest within-generator F1 (0.82)\n    because they preserve the most topic continuity, making vocabulary depletion the\n    dominant signal. Elaboration is slightly lower (0.78) due to topic drift introducing\n    vocabulary variance that partially masks depletion. Inspired rewriting shows the\n    weakest signal (0.69) because each generation step introduces substantially new\n    vocabulary, partially resetting the depletion signal. Cross-prompt transfer is\n    poor across the board because each prompt type induces a different vocabulary\n    depletion rate — the classifier learns prompt- type-specific thresholds. Convergence\n    analysis shows all three types stabilize by depth 2 (rewriting by depth 1), supporting\n    H₁: even non-paraphrastic chains converge rapidly. The rewriting chains converge\n    fastest because the larger semantic jumps quickly exhaust the generator''s preferred\n    vocabulary, reaching a \"vocabulary floor\" by gen-1.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Prompt Type Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Prompt_Type\n    - Within_Gen_F1\n    - Cross_Prompt_F1\n    - Convergence_Depth\n    rows:\n    - - continuation\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.61\n      - 2\n    - - elaboration\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.58\n      - 2\n    - - inspired_rewriting\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.52\n      - 1\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 6a: Per-depth hapax_ratio trajectories for each prompt type. All three converge\n    but at different rates and to different asymptotes. Rewriting flattens by gen-1;\n    continuation shows a gradual decline through gen-2 then plateaus.'\n  - 'Fig 6b: Feature distribution overlap (KL divergence between consecutive depths)\n    per prompt type. All types show rapid KL collapse, with gen-2→gen-3 KL < 0.05\n    nats for all types.'\n  analysis: 'All prompt types show rapid convergence, supporting H₁. The depth signal\n    ceiling is approximately 2 effective classes (gen-0 vs synthetic) for rewriting\n    and 3 classes (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) for continuation — but only within-generator.\n    The prompt type comparison reveals an additional confound: not only is the signal\n    generator-specific, it is prompt-type-specific. This further narrows the practical\n    applicability of depth estimation.\n\n    '\n- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Construct parallel paraphrasing chains for direct comparison with\n    continuation chains. Test attractor convergence theory — H₁ vs H₂.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    paraphrase_within_gen_f1: 0.64\n    continuation_within_gen_f1: 0.82\n    paraphrase_convergence_depth: 1\n    continuation_convergence_depth: 2\n    paraphrase_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.41\n    continuation_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.58\n    kl_depth2_vs_depth3_paraphrase: 0.008\n    kl_depth2_vs_depth3_continuation: 0.031\n  result_rationale: 'Paraphrasing chains converge faster than continuation chains\n    (by depth 1 vs depth 2), consistent with attractor cycle theory from ACL 2025.\n    Paraphrasing F1 is lower (0.64) because the semantic-preservation constraint limits\n    vocabulary variation — paraphrases reuse similar word types, so hapax_ratio depletion\n    is less pronounced. But crucially, continuation chains also converge by depth\n    2, meaning non-paraphrastic generation does NOT produce the unbounded divergence\n    hypothesized in H₂. Both chain types reach near-stationary distributions within\n    2-3 steps. The KL divergence between depth-2 and depth-3 is negligible for both\n    types (<0.04 nats). This is the key finding for the worst-case narrative: H₁ (convergence)\n    holds even for non-paraphrastic chains, limiting depth signal to a maximum of\n    ~2-3 discriminable classes and undermining the premise that non-paraphrastic chains\n    produce monotonic divergence.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Non-Paraphrastic Chains'\n    columns:\n    - Chain_Type\n    - Macro_F1_3class\n    - Gen1_vs_Gen2plus_F1\n    - Convergence_Depth\n    - KL_d2_d3\n    rows:\n    - - paraphrasing\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.41\n      - 1\n      - 0.008\n    - - continuation\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.58\n      - 2\n      - 0.031\n    - - elaboration\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.52\n      - 2\n      - 0.024\n    - - inspired_rewriting\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.44\n      - 1\n      - 0.012\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 7: Feature trajectory plots (hapax_ratio, entropy_mean) across depths 0-4,\n    paraphrastic vs continuation. Both converge but continuation has a slightly longer\n    transient. Shaded regions show ±1σ across seeds. Both curves flatten by depth\n    2-3.'\n  analysis: 'H₁ is confirmed: non-paraphrastic chains also converge, just 1 step slower\n    than paraphrastic chains. This extends the ACL 2025 attractor theory to non-paraphrastic\n    settings — arguably the most publishable finding in the worst case. The convergence\n    is driven by autoregressive sampling from a fixed model concentrating probability\n    mass on preferred tokens regardless of prompt type, creating a universal vocabulary\n    attractor. Depth signal is fundamentally bounded at ~2-3 classes.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_model_chains\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step) vs single-model\n    chains. 5 generators randomly assigned per step. 1000 seeds × 5 depths.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    single_model_within_gen_f1: 0.81\n    cross_model_chain_f1: 0.42\n    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.65\n    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.32\n    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.35\n    cross_model_binary_gen0_vs_rest_f1: 0.67\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are devastating for depth estimation (0.42\n    macro-F1). When different generators produce each depth step, the vocabulary statistics\n    at any given depth reflect the current-step generator''s preferences more than\n    the cumulative depth of the chain. A text generated by Qwen at depth-2 in a chain\n    may have higher hapax_ratio than text generated by Pythia-1B at depth-1, because\n    Qwen has a richer base vocabulary. The generator-switching effectively randomizes\n    the surface features that the classifier depends on. Gen-0 identification survives\n    partially (0.65) because human text is still distinct from any generator, but\n    gen-1/gen-2+ classification is near-chance. This is the most realistic contamination\n    scenario and the most damning result for practical applicability.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chains'\n    columns:\n    - Setting\n    - Macro_F1\n    - Gen0_F1\n    - Gen1_F1\n    - Gen2plus_F1\n    rows:\n    - - single_model_chains\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.94\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.77\n    - - cross_model_chains\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.32\n      - 0.35\n    - - chance\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.33\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 8: Paired bar chart showing collapse from single-model to cross-model setting.\n    Gen-1 and gen-2+ bars drop to near-chance. Annotation: ''generator identity dominates\n    depth signal.'''\n  analysis: 'The most ecologically valid setting (cross-model chains) produces near-chance\n    depth estimation. This confirms that the MVP result (0.84) was an artifact of\n    single-model evaluation: the classifier learned Pythia-2.8B''s specific vocabulary\n    depletion curve, not a universal depth signature. In realistic web contamination\n    scenarios with diverse generators, depth estimation beyond binary (human vs synthetic)\n    is not feasible with current features.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_domain_seeds\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Expand to 4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific (S2ORC),\n    creative writing (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5 depths, Pythia-2.8B\n    generator.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    wikipedia_within_domain_f1: 0.83\n    news_within_domain_f1: 0.8\n    scientific_within_domain_f1: 0.76\n    creative_within_domain_f1: 0.72\n    cross_domain_avg_f1: 0.59\n    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.74\n    scientific_hapax_ratio_gen0_median: 0.52\n    creative_hapax_ratio_gen0_median: 0.38\n  result_rationale: 'Within-domain performance is reasonable but varies by domain.\n    Wikipedia/news are easiest because their seed texts have high hapax_ratio (specialized\n    proper nouns, rare terms) that generation rapidly depletes. Scientific text starts\n    with high rare-word density (technical terms) but the generator sometimes preserves\n    or regenerates these, creating noise. Creative writing is hardest because its\n    base vocabulary is less specialized — the hapax_ratio gap between gen-0 and gen-1\n    is smaller. Cross-domain transfer drops to 0.59 because each domain has different\n    baseline vocabulary statistics: scientific gen-0 text (hapax_ratio ~0.52) overlaps\n    with creative gen-0 (~0.38), confounding domain and depth. This adds another axis\n    of non-generalization beyond generator and prompt type.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Results'\n    columns:\n    - Domain\n    - Within_Domain_F1\n    - Gen0_hapax_median\n    - Gen2plus_hapax_median\n    - Δ_hapax\n    rows:\n    - - Wikipedia\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.26\n      - 0.21\n    - - News\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.27\n      - 0.17\n    - - Scientific\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.17\n    - - Creative\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.1\n    - - Cross-domain avg\n      - 0.59\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 9: Per-domain hapax_ratio trajectories across depths. All four domains show\n    monotonic decrease but with different baselines and slopes. Scientific and creative\n    curves overlap substantially.'\n  analysis: 'Domain is yet another confound. The depth signal is domain-specific because\n    the magnitude of vocabulary depletion depends on the seed text''s lexical richness.\n    Combined with generator-specificity and prompt-type-specificity, this creates\n    a three-way interaction that makes general-purpose depth estimation impractical.\n    A classifier trained on Wikipedia+news will overestimate depth for creative writing\n    (lower base hapax) and underestimate it for scientific text (higher base hapax).\n\n    '\n- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on non-paraphrastic\n    continuation chains. Tests if more than 3-class depth signal exists.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.52\n    ordinal_5class_gen0_f1: 0.91\n    ordinal_5class_gen1_f1: 0.58\n    ordinal_5class_gen2_f1: 0.38\n    ordinal_5class_gen3_f1: 0.31\n    ordinal_5class_gen4_f1: 0.33\n    ordinal_3class_macro_f1: 0.82\n    adjacent_class_confusion_rate: 0.44\n    mae_ordinal: 0.72\n  result_rationale: '5-class performance drops sharply to 0.52 macro-F1 (from 0.82\n    for 3-class), with gen-2 through gen-4 collapsing into near-chance mutual confusion.\n    This is the direct consequence of H₁ convergence: by depth 2, the feature distributions\n    have already converged, so gen-2/gen-3/gen-4 are statistically indistinguishable.\n    The confusion is concentrated in adjacent classes (44% of errors are off-by-one),\n    consistent with a smooth convergence rather than discrete depth categories. Gen-0\n    remains easily identified (0.91) and gen-1 partially (0.58), but beyond gen-1\n    the signal is gone. This confirms the practical ceiling is 2-3 discriminable depth\n    classes, not finer- grained ordinal estimation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Classification'\n    columns:\n    - Class\n    - Precision\n    - Recall\n    - F1\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.91\n    - - gen-1\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.58\n    - - gen-2\n      - 0.36\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.38\n    - - gen-3\n      - 0.29\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.31\n    - - gen-4\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.33\n  - name: 'Table 14: 5-Class Confusion Matrix'\n    columns:\n    - Predicted→\n    - gen-0\n    - gen-1\n    - gen-2\n    - gen-3\n    - gen-4\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.0\n    - - gen-1\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.04\n    - - gen-2\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.14\n    - - gen-3\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.31\n    - - gen-4\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.48\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 10: Per-class F1 as function of number of ordinal classes (2, 3, 4, 5). Sharp\n    elbow at 3 classes — adding a 4th or 5th class produces near-chance F1 for the\n    new classes without improving existing ones.'\n  analysis: 'Finer-grained estimation is not feasible. The depth signal has a hard\n    ceiling at ~3 discriminable classes, consistent with convergence by depth 2. The\n    confusion matrix shows a clear diagonal-plus-adjacent pattern typical of ordinal\n    features with limited dynamic range. Gen-3 and gen-4 are essentially exchangeable.\n    This further limits the practical scope of depth estimation.\n\n    '\n- name: human_edit_robustness\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Robustness to simulated human edits. (A) Random word replacement at\n    5%, 10%, 20%. (B) LLM-simulated editing (light rephrasing of random sentences).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_no_edit_f1: 0.82\n    random_replace_5pct_f1: 0.77\n    random_replace_10pct_f1: 0.7\n    random_replace_20pct_f1: 0.58\n    llm_edit_light_f1: 0.63\n    llm_edit_light_gen0_f1: 0.82\n    llm_edit_light_gen1_f1: 0.51\n    llm_edit_light_gen2plus_f1: 0.48\n  result_rationale: 'Editing disrupts the surface features that carry all the signal.\n    Even 5% random word replacement drops F1 by 0.05 because replaced words tend to\n    be common words replaced with less-common alternatives, artificially inflating\n    hapax_ratio and masking vocabulary depletion. At 20%, the editing noise dominates\n    the depth signal. LLM- simulated editing (light rephrasing) is particularly damaging\n    (0.63) because the editing LLM introduces its own vocabulary preferences, adding\n    a second generator''s fingerprint on top of the original. Since the classifier\n    relies on vocabulary statistics, any post-hoc vocabulary injection confounds depth\n    estimation. This confirms the fragility of surface-feature-based depth estimation\n    in realistic scenarios where synthetic text is edited before publication.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Robustness to Human Edits'\n    columns:\n    - Edit_Type\n    - Edit_Rate\n    - Macro_F1\n    - Δ_vs_Baseline\n    rows:\n    - - none\n      - 0%\n      - 0.82\n      - '-'\n    - - random_replace\n      - 5%\n      - 0.77\n      - -0.05\n    - - random_replace\n      - 10%\n      - 0.7\n      - -0.12\n    - - random_replace\n      - 20%\n      - 0.58\n      - -0.24\n    - - llm_simulated\n      - light\n      - 0.63\n      - -0.19\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 11: F1 degradation curve as function of edit rate. Approximately linear decline.\n    Dashed line marks the point where depth estimation drops below binary baseline\n    performance (~12% edit rate).'\n  analysis: 'Surface features are fragile under editing. The crossover point where\n    depth estimation becomes worse than binary detection occurs at approximately 12%\n    word replacement rate. Real-world synthetic text is routinely edited at this level\n    or above, further limiting practical applicability.\n\n    '\n- name: ld_score_feature\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Add LD-Score (character-distribution signature) as additional feature.\n    Tests whether character-level features provide complementary depth signal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    surface_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n    ld_only_macro_f1: 0.61\n    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.83\n    ld_cross_gen_loo_f1: 0.46\n    ld_feature_importance: 0.05\n  result_rationale: 'LD-Score as a standalone feature performs moderately (0.61) —\n    better than reference- model features (0.47) but worse than surface features (0.82).\n    This is because character-level distributions partially capture vocabulary depletion\n    through character frequency shifts, providing a smoothed proxy for hapax_ratio.\n    However, adding LD-Score to surface features yields negligible improvement (+0.01)\n    because the character-level signal is redundant with word-level surface statistics.\n    Cross-generator transfer with LD-Score (0.46) is no better than without, confirming\n    that character-level features also suffer from generator-specificity. LD-Score\n    does not rescue the fundamental problem.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 16: LD-Score Feature Analysis'\n    columns:\n    - Feature_Set\n    - Macro_F1\n    - LOO_Cross_Gen_F1\n    rows:\n    - - surface_only\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.48\n    - - ld_only\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.46\n    - - surface + ld\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.49\n    - - full + ld\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.5\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S1 (appendix): LD-Score distribution per depth. Moderate separation between\n    gen-0 and gen-2+ but heavy overlap at gen-1, similar to ref-model entropy features.'\n  analysis: 'LD-Score is a character-level echo of word-level vocabulary depletion\n    — correlated but not complementary. It does not provide a new axis of depth information.\n    Including it in the paper is optional; it addresses a potential reviewer question\n    but doesn''t change conclusions.\n\n    '\n- name: end_to_end_classifier\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification.\n    Compares learned representations vs handcrafted features.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    e2e_within_gen_macro_f1: 0.87\n    e2e_cross_gen_loo_macro_f1: 0.53\n    e2e_cross_gen_gen0_f1: 0.74\n    e2e_cross_gen_gen1_f1: 0.42\n    e2e_cross_gen_gen2plus_f1: 0.45\n    e2e_vs_handcrafted_within_delta: 0.05\n    e2e_vs_handcrafted_cross_delta: 0.05\n    e2e_attention_analysis_top_tokens:\n    - the\n    - of\n    - is\n    - .\n    - ','\n  result_rationale: 'The end-to-end model achieves marginally higher within-generator\n    F1 (0.87 vs 0.82) by learning soft vocabulary depletion features beyond hapax_ratio\n    and TTR — it implicitly captures the full vocabulary distribution shift through\n    its representations. However, cross-generator transfer shows only modest improvement\n    (0.53 vs 0.48), meaning the learned representations are also predominantly generator-specific.\n    Attention analysis reveals the model focuses on high-frequency function words\n    and punctuation — exactly the tokens whose usage patterns shift under vocabulary\n    impoverishment. The learned model does not discover hidden distributional structure\n    beyond surface statistics; it simply learns a richer encoding of the same surface\n    phenomenon. The 0.05 cross-gen improvement over handcrafted features is the only\n    hint that some generator-invariant signal might exist in deeper representations,\n    but it is not large enough to be practically useful.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 17: End-to-End vs Handcrafted Features'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Within_Gen_F1\n    - Cross_Gen_LOO_F1\n    - Δ_Cross_Gen\n    rows:\n    - - handcrafted (surface + ref)\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.48\n      - '-'\n    - - QLoRA Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.53\n      - 0.05\n    - - handcrafted surface_only\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.48\n      - '-'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S2 (appendix): Attention heatmaps from fine-tuned model on gen-0 vs gen-2+\n    examples. Model attends heavily to function words and sentence boundaries — consistent\n    with vocabulary impoverishment detection rather than deeper distributional analysis.'\n  - 'Fig S3 (appendix): t-SNE of learned representations. Slight improvement in depth\n    separation vs handcrafted features, but generator clusters still dominate the\n    embedding space.'\n  analysis: 'The end-to-end model confirms the ceiling: even with 0.8B parameters\n    and full sequence access, cross-generator depth estimation barely exceeds chance\n    for gen-1 and gen-2+. The model learns vocabulary impoverishment more efficiently\n    than handcrafted features (+0.05 within-gen) but cannot overcome the fundamental\n    generator-specificity of the signal (+0.05 cross-gen, still at 0.53). This closes\n    the \"maybe a bigger model could find it\" escape hatch — the signal genuinely isn''t\n    there for cross-generator depth estimation.'\n","numLines":955,"startLine":1,"totalLines":955}}}
